{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואת תדבר", :translit "v'at tidaber", :gloss "and you (fem.) speak"} {:he "בקול נמוך", :translit "b'kol namuch", :gloss "in a low voice"} {:he "כאשה", :translit "k'ishah", :gloss "like a woman"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Feminine form 'ואת' triggers reading: God spoke to Israel with a soft, attenuated voice"}], :frame {:text "The word ואת is grammatically feminine, and Ba'al HaTurim reads this as a hint that God's voice at Sinai was made soft so Israel could withstand it. The Talmud describes that at Sinai God's voice was gentle, as it is written: 'The voice of the LORD is in power' (Psalms 29:4) — not 'in His power' but 'in power,' meaning it was adjusted according to each person's strength (Berakhot 45a). The comparison to a woman draws on the idiom of a soft or gentle voice.", :sources ["Berakhot 45a" "Psalms 29:4"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ואת תדבר", :translit "v'at tidaber", :gloss "and you (fem.) speak"} {:he "שמונה דברות", :translit "shemonah dibrot", :gloss "eight commandments / utterances"} {:he "דברות", :translit "dibrot", :gloss "commandments / utterances"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ואת תדבר", :b "שמונה דברות"}], :frame {:text "The Israelites say to Moses, 'You speak with us... and you speak' (Deuteronomy 5:24–27). A Rabbinic tradition holds that the Israelites heard the first two Decalogue statements directly from God, and the remaining eight were communicated through Moses, corresponding to the people's request that Moses mediate. The gematria of 'ואת תדבר' equals 'שמונה דברות' (eight commandments), pointing to these final eight that Moses would relay.", :sources ["Makkot 24a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Feminine form 'ואת' triggers reading: God spoke to Israel with a soft, attenuated voice", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "ואת תדבר", :a-value 1013, :b "שמונה דברות", :b-value 1013, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:34:40.314001677Z", :model "glm-5.2"}